          "id": "sec-1-1",
          "secNumber": "1.1",
          "heading": "Foundational Principles & Theory: Bases, Stream Definitions and Conservation Laws",
          "content": "A defensible balance begins with a basis, boundary, property definition and stream table. This unit makes units and conserved quantities explicit before solving unknown flow rates.\n\n## Core learning goals\n\n- Explain and apply **Process quantities and calculation bases**, including assumptions and independent verification.\n- Explain and apply **Component balances and degrees of freedom**, including assumptions and independent verification.\n- Explain and apply **Mass, mole and concentration reporting**, including assumptions and independent verification.\n- Explain and apply **Boundary selection and closure diagnostics**, including assumptions and independent verification.\n\n## Lessons and reading order\n\n- [Lesson 1.1: Process quantities and calculation bases](../lessons/unit-01-lesson-01.html)\n- [Lesson 1.2: Component balances and degrees of freedom](../lessons/unit-01-lesson-02.html)\n- [Lesson 1.3: Mass, mole and concentration reporting](../lessons/unit-01-lesson-03.html)\n- [Lesson 1.4: Boundary selection and closure diagnostics](../lessons/unit-01-lesson-04.html)\n\n## Unit synthesis: identify what is known\n\nA defensible balance begins with a basis, boundary, property definition and stream table. This unit makes units and conserved quantities explicit before solving unknown flow rates. A strong solution begins with a process sketch and a stream table. Write values to a stated basis, mark all missing data, and calculate degrees of freedom before attempting numerical iteration. A flow diagram is useful only if the stream symbols and assumptions can be traced into actual balance equations. If an unknown variable is introduced to close a balance, explain what physical instrument or property measurement would establish it.\n\n### Connecting the four lessons\n\nThe first lesson establishes a local quantity or concept, while the later lessons expose how its interpretation changes with additional process complexity. For example, mass fractions remain valid for a blend but reacting compositions need generation terms; heat duties may be linear in temperature under constant heat capacity but nonlinear near a phase transition. Develop a single reference stream table and compare how each analytical step changes when more units are added.\n\n### Diagnostic exercise\n\nAssume an otherwise plausible design produces an impossible negative outlet or a reported overall recovery of 105%. Instead of clipping the number, trace which equation or boundary was omitted or whose units were inconsistent. Check whether the solver was overdetermined by a redundant balance or underdetermined by a missing composition. Document the correction and rerun your limiting-case tests.\n\nA second diagnostic asks you to separate *measured* and *assumed* quantities. Label uncertainties if known; do not attach precision to idealized coefficients. A balance audit should preserve the original measurements and record any reconciliation separately. This habit will become important in the integrated case studies and final project.\n\n### Unit completion exercise\n\nDraw one example flowsheet for this unit and formulate an overall balance plus two meaningful independent additional equations, if the process contains enough components. State why each equation is independent. Determine what unknowns remain and choose the cheapest credible measurement to close the problem. Explain whether the modified process is still steady, and assess one alternative operating condition without assuming unchanged performance."
