          "secNumber": "1.7",
          "heading": "Engineering Audit, Diagnostic Reasoning & Assessment: Bases, Stream Definitions and Conservation Laws",
          "content": "# Assessment \u2014 Unit 1: Bases, stream definitions and conservation\n\nThese questions demand a calculation or a defended explanation. State units and assumptions for every numerical answer.\n\n## Topic 1.1: Process quantities and calculation bases\n\n1. **Derivation.** Build a balance for: Moisture-sensitive food granules; report wet and dry bases side by side. Derive the expression `x_wet=m_water/(m_water+m_dry); X_dry=m_water/m_dry; X_dry=x_wet/(1-x_wet)` or explain why an additional relationship is necessary.\n2. **Quantitative check.** Recalculate this illustrative scenario without quoting the reported result: A dryer sample has 12 kg water and 48 kg dry solids; wet-basis moisture is 20% and dry-basis moisture is 25%. Record a self-check based on conservation.\n3. **Error diagnosis.** A colleague proposes an answer that contradicts this principle: Focusing only on a nominal feed flow can conceal basis mismatch, especially when a result is reported per kilogram of dry solids. Identify the failed assumption and describe a corrective measurement.\n4. **Sensitivity.** Change the most influential given input by +10%, leaving other inputs fixed. Determine the new result where the data suffice; otherwise explicitly name the missing information.\n5. **Boundary test.** Present a physically meaningful limit and show how the predicted result behaves. State whether a thermodynamic property correlation remains valid in that limit.\n\n## Topic 1.2: Component balances and degrees of freedom\n\n1. **Derivation.** Build a balance for: Dye-house water reuse; identify which composition measurement closes the system. Derive the expression `sum(out_i)-sum(in_i)=generation_i-accumulation_i; at steady nonreactive conditions sum(out_i)=sum(in_i)` or explain why an additional relationship is necessary.\n2. **Quantitative check.** Recalculate this illustrative scenario without quoting the reported result: A 140 kg/h liquid stream at 30 wt% solute contains 42 kg/h solute; dilution to 12 wt% requires total 350 kg/h and 210 kg/h water. Record a self-check based on conservation.\n3. **Error diagnosis.** A colleague proposes an answer that contradicts this principle: Counting an overall balance and every component balance as independent adds one redundant equation. Identify the failed assumption and describe a corrective measurement.\n4. **Sensitivity.** Change the most influential given input by +10%, leaving other inputs fixed. Determine the new result where the data suffice; otherwise explicitly name the missing information.\n5. **Boundary test.** Present a physically meaningful limit and show how the predicted result behaves. State whether a thermodynamic property correlation remains valid in that limit.\n\n## Topic 1.3: Mass, mole and concentration reporting\n\n1. **Derivation.** Build a balance for: Solvent blending; compare kg solute per kg mixture with mol solute per mol mixture. Derive the expression `x_i=(w_i/M_i)/sum_j(w_j/M_j); M_mix=sum_i(x_i M_i)` or explain why an additional relationship is necessary.\n2. **Quantitative check.** Recalculate this illustrative scenario without quoting the reported result: A 46 kg ethanol and 54 kg water charge corresponds to approximately 1 kmol ethanol and 3 kmol water; ethanol mole fraction is 0.25. Record a self-check based on conservation.\n3. **Error diagnosis.** A colleague proposes an answer that contradicts this principle: Converting mass percentage directly into mole percentage is wrong unless the molecular weights coincide. Identify the failed assumption and describe a corrective measurement.\n4. **Sensitivity.** Change the most influential given input by +10%, leaving other inputs fixed. Determine the new result where the data suffice; otherwise explicitly name the missing information.\n5. **Boundary test.** Present a physically meaningful limit and show how the predicted result behaves. State whether a thermodynamic property correlation remains valid in that limit.\n\n## Topic 1.4: Boundary selection and closure diagnostics\n\n1. **Derivation.** Build a balance for: Plant audit; quantify balance closure with and without uncertain off-gas. Derive the expression `closure_error=sum(in)-sum(out)-accumulation+generation` or explain why an additional relationship is necessary.\n2. **Quantitative check.** Recalculate this illustrative scenario without quoting the reported result: A plant records 1250 kg/h in and 1215 kg/h out with zero change of inventory; 35 kg/h is unexplained and must be investigated, not assigned automatically to evaporation. Record a self-check based on conservation.\n3. **Error diagnosis.** A colleague proposes an answer that contradicts this principle: Instrumentation error and unmeasured vents cannot be distinguished from a single overall closure number. Identify the failed assumption and describe a corrective measurement.\n4. **Sensitivity.** Change the most influential given input by +10%, leaving other inputs fixed. Determine the new result where the data suffice; otherwise explicitly name the missing information.\n5. **Boundary test.** Present a physically meaningful limit and show how the predicted result behaves. State whether a thermodynamic property correlation remains valid in that limit.\n\n## Grading rubric\n\nAward credit for a correctly defined boundary and flow basis (25%), an equation with independent unknowns (25%), numerical units and magnitude checks (20%), critical assumptions and uncertainty (20%), and readable presentation (10%). More digits are not a substitute for property data."
