          "content": "# Unit 6.4: Pumps, compressors and flow work\n\n**Book 2: Material and Energy Balances \u2014 Energy balances on process equipment**\n\n## What you will be able to do\n\nShaft power and fluid enthalpy increase are linked by efficiency and flow work. Interpret **P_hydraulic=rho*g*Q_vol*H; P_shaft=P_hydraulic/eta** and demonstrate how it follows from an appropriate boundary. Identify whether the equation describes an overall, species, elemental, or energy balance; discuss what is actually measured and the conditions under which the balance is valid.\n\n## Essential physical insight\n\nPump retrofit with measured head, flow and electrical power. The critical objective is not to memorize a plug-in formula but to recognize conserved quantities and define the physical process correctly. Break the problem into stream variables, known specifications, unknowns, and constitutive or property relationships. A numerical estimate is an engineering claim only if its basis and limitations are reproducible.\n\n### Deriving the balance from the process boundary\n\nStart from the observations implied by **pumps, compressors and flow work**. Shaft power and fluid enthalpy increase are linked by efficiency and flow work. Represent each material crossing the boundary with a signed rate and state its measurement basis. If the equipment retains material, include an accumulation term. A balance written per second cannot be directly combined with a plant log written per shift without converting the interval; similarly, mass fractions and molar compositions cannot be used in the same species account without a molecular-weight transformation. Before any algebra, list feed states, outlet states, any generation or consumption, and the measured inventory change.\n\nWrite the central relationship as **P_hydraulic=rho*g*Q_vol*H; P_shaft=P_hydraulic/eta**. Read every variable aloud with its units, explaining which terms are independent measurements and which are derived. For a process with reaction, the component generation term is not optional. For a nonreactive mixer, a nonzero computed generation indicates a modeling or measurement mistake. Use independent balance closure as the first diagnostic check, and then compare the predicted result against simple physical limits.\n\n### A worked calculation and its interpretation\n\n**Illustrative case.** For water at 1000 kg/m^3, 0.02 m^3/s and 30 m head, power delivered to water is 5.886 kW; at efficiency 0.75 input is 7.848 kW. This numerical calculation is a worked illustration and not a measurement from an operating facility. Write the stream table with at least one named component, a physical total rate, and a units column. Substitute numbers into the governing equation and solve the unknown before rounding. Keep at least one more significant figure in intermediate calculations than will be reported to the operator.\n\nThe numerical result has value only within the stated assumptions. Do not divide by efficiency when calculating useful fluid power. Confirm that any calculated component fraction is between zero and one, that recoveries never exceed their admissible range and that energy duties obey the stated sign convention. A unit conversion error can shift a prediction by factors of 1,000 or 3,600 even when the arithmetic appears internally consistent.\n\n### Why the answer changes in a real plant\n\nConsider the industrial case: Pump retrofit with measured head, flow and electrical power. Before trusting a software result, identify temperature and pressure effects, instrument calibration, inventory fluctuations and unrecorded process streams. A perfectly balanced flowsheet can still be a poor representation of reality if one instrument reads systematically low. Conversely, a balance residual does not by itself prove a physical leak or chemical loss.\n\nTo decide whether further detail is necessary, first quantify the decision at issue. A rough balance may be sufficient to choose between two pilot runs but not to specify relief equipment, guarantee product quality, or assert environmental compliance. Explain which property or stream measurement would most reduce uncertainty, and how it could be obtained without introducing a new uncontrolled hazard.\n\n### Guided redesign exercise\n\nImagine a proposed 20% increase in the process feed or load. Identify which terms in **P_hydraulic=rho*g*Q_vol*H; P_shaft=P_hydraulic/eta** scale linearly and which could change because of capacity limits, residence time, equilibrium, or heat transfer. Recompute the balance on a consistent time basis and explain why unchanged efficiency or composition may be an unreasonable assumption. Do not silently extrapolate a laboratory coefficient outside the range where it was measured.\n\nPrepare a second scenario in which the least certain input changes by 10% while the other stated conditions remain fixed. Estimate whether the output changes proportionally. If the result violates a conservation limit, retrace variable definitions. If it remains feasible, identify an observation that could distinguish between the original and changed scenarios.\n\n### Verification and critical-thinking questions\n\n1. State the system boundary and draw the streams for pumps, compressors and flow work; label every unknown rather than assigning guessed values.\n2. Explain the difference between an identity enforced by conservation and an empirical correlation that may fail at changed conditions.\n3. What measurement would most directly test the assumption: shaft power and fluid enthalpy increase are linked by efficiency and flow work?\n4. Recompute the illustrative case using a different but consistent calculation basis. Which outputs must remain invariant?\n5. Construct a limiting-case test and explain precisely what would constitute a physically impossible result.\n\n**Practical check:** Do not divide by efficiency when calculating useful fluid power. A satisfactory answer should separate the equation, numerical result, process interpretation, and limitations rather than ending with a number alone.\n\n\n## Practical laboratory connection\n\nThe laboratory models in this volume can illustrate how a calculated response changes as conditions vary. Locate a related simulation in the book index, write down a prediction before operating its controls, and test whether the result is monotone, bounded or approaching an asymptotic limit. Compare at least one displayed numerical result with an independent calculation. A graphical match is not sufficient: test numerical values and units separately.\n\n## Further study\n\nConsult the freely accessible LearnChemE Material and Energy Balances resources and the NIST Chemistry WebBook for property-data methodology. Use a suitable authoritative property model for real design rather than applying constant heat capacities or ideal-mixture assumptions indiscriminately."
